I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, diffraction grid, and the reusable refinement utilities first. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible phase inventory, test candidate structures against the pattern, and only refine complexity that the residuals support.

The chemistry points first to calcium tungstates, but I’m treating precursor remnants and alternate Ca–W–O stoichiometries/polymorphs as real candidates rather than assuming single-phase CaWO₄. I’m now extracting peak positions and querying the supplied structure index for exact records and physical alternates.

The peak families already separate cleanly into scheelite CaWO₄ (space group I4₁/a) and room-temperature monoclinic WO₃: the three WO₃ lines near 23–24.2° and their 33–34°, 41–42°, and 50–56° families are not explainable by CaWO₄. A common ≈−0.16° angular offset appears across both phases, so I’ll model one shared correction before changing cells. CaO remains on the weak-phase watch list because its 37.3° family may be present, but its companion lines need a joint test.

The first two-phase refinement is running on the full, unresampled 8,301-point grid. I’ve fixed unused size/strain, roughness, and texture terms at neutral values and am refining only background, phase scales, and the shared angular offset at this stage.

The common-profile challenge rejected Gaussian U and W: both collapsed to zero while the Lorentzian X term remained positive and materially improved the fit. I’m pruning those bound terms now and replaying from a clean checkpoint with only X active, as required; no bound/frozen variables will be retained.

The first batch over-parallelized the numerical backend and hit a thread-resource limit; several trials did complete, but I won’t use partial results. I’m reducing concurrency and forcing one BLAS thread per trial, then I’ll compare only fully converged branches.

The source-record screen is now narrowed to physically credible WO₃ polymorphs. I’m comparing the ambient monoclinic records against the triclinic and orthorhombic alternatives under the identical six-term background, X-only profile, angular correction, and refinement sequence; the high-pressure CaWO₄ record and malformed 1931 duplicate-site record are excluded on source/geometry grounds.

The angle-dependent intensity correction is better represented by refined polarization than by the one-parameter roughness alternative once texture is challenged: polarization + CaWO₄ [110] March–Dollase gives Rwp ≈10.29%, versus ≈10.51% for roughness + [110]. Simultaneously refining both is strongly correlated (|r| up to 0.96 with scale), so I’m rejecting that combined surrogate model. I’m now re-testing CaO, Ca(OH)₂, and CaCO₃ against the clean polarization/[110] checkpoint.

The two-phase inventory is now stable; CaO improves only the 32°/37° residuals while worsening its expected 53.6°, 63.9°, 67.1°, and 88.2° families, and its free cell drifts without convergence. I’m therefore leaving CaO on the watch list rather than reporting a spurious ~2% fraction. Next I’m comparing phase-specific neutral/size/strain/combined broadening from the same checkpoint.

The broadening tests are replay-stable: CaWO₄ requires isotropic size (~0.13 μm) plus strain, and the strain is significantly anisotropic along [001] (equatorial ≈395 με, axial ≈2060 με). Making size anisotropic adds only ~0.0014 Rwp percentage point once strain anisotropy is present, so I’m pruning that extra term. WO₃ size/strain collapses to the neutral limit. I’m doing the final weak-phase re-challenge against this profile before coordinate testing.

The exact-record weak-phase checks do not establish a third phase: CaO’s nominal scale is driven mainly by two residual regions and contradicts most of its companion families; Ca(OH)₂ is only ~2σ and calcite collapses to zero. Because the precursor ratio is unknown, I’m also following the supplied analogue route for Ca-rich tungstates—especially isovalent Ca₃OsO₆→Ca₃WO₆ and Ba₂ReO₅→Ca₂WO₅ prototypes—before closing the inventory.

The analogue screen resolved the residual inventory decisively. Isovalent Ca₃OsO₆→Ca₃WO₆ (P2₁/n parent, Os⁶⁺→W⁶⁺) accounts for the coherent 18.9/19.5°, 22.2°, 30.8–32.3°, 36.7–37.7°, 45.2°, 49–51°, and 55–57° families; Rwp falls from 9.43% to 5.47%, with ~10 wt% scale. The U-parent polymorph goes to zero. Because the inventory changed, I’m now resetting phase broadening/texture and rebuilding the common profile before accepting fractions or testing any fourth phase.

With the corrected three-phase inventory, the common profile changes materially: W and X are supported, U is not; SH/L again collapses. Low-angle roughness is now clearly favored over polarization (Rwp 7.38% vs 8.08%), showing that the missing Ca₃WO₆ had previously biased that decision. CaWO₄ [110] texture remains the best of six tested axes. I’m checking whether the Ca₃WO₆ analogue itself has supported orientation before phase-specific widths are rebuilt.

The three-phase inventory is now coherent. I’m finishing the remaining symmetry-aware broadening challenges, then I’ll re-test all weak-phase candidates against that common profile before the mandated heavy-atom coordinate pass and exact final export.

The anisotropy challenge is negative: uniaxial size improves Rwp by only 0.00086 percentage point with nearly equal axial/equatorial sizes, while uniaxial strain slightly worsens it. I’m retaining the simpler isotropic size-plus-strain model and now replaying the CaO, Ca(OH)₂, CaCO₃, and Ca₂WO₅ watch list from that same checkpoint.

Three watch-list candidates are unsupported: hydroxide and carbonate scales collapse to zero; the Ca₂WO₅ analogue gives only 0.10(4) wt%, a 0.0013-point Rwp change, and fails stable replay. CaO remains the only borderline case at 0.32(5) wt%; I’m checking whether its full rocksalt reflection family is present, rather than accepting two overlapped low-angle residuals as a trace phase.

CaO fails the family-coherence test: it improves the 111/200 windows but makes the stronger expected 220 window substantially worse and does not consistently improve the 311/222/331/420 companions. I’m therefore keeping it off the modeled inventory (with its 0.32 wt% trial as the trace-detection uncertainty), preparing the converged three-phase checkpoint, and then running the required one-site-at-a-time heavy-coordinate challenge.

The starting checkpoint reopens cleanly (Rwp 3.9935%, no frozen variables, maximum shift/esd 0.010). The mandated coordinate helper is now testing each Ca/W site independently with its displacement, contact, and minimum-gain guards; I’ll only adopt its output if the accepted gain survives a fresh reopen.

The coordinate pass accepted three physically bounded moves: both monoclinic WO₃ W sites (0.076 and 0.066 Å) and one Ca site in the Ca₃WO₆ prototype (0.050 Å). Rwp fell from 3.9935% to 3.8870%, no variables froze, and all phases passed contact checks; all other heavy sites failed the gain threshold. I’m now doing the independent reopen/replay check before adopting this project.

After coordinate refinement, the CaO trial drops to 0.15(5) wt% and only a 0.0018-point Rwp change. The Ca₂WO₅ prototype appears at 0.18(4) wt%, but its cell-refined trial is unstable (maximum shift/esd 0.70). I’m separating scale support from cell drift now; a trace analogue will not be reported as a phase unless multiple families survive a fixed, physically screened model and stable replay.

The fixed-cell Ca₂WO₅ test is only 0.11(4) wt% with a 0.0015-point Rwp gain; releasing its cell causes unstable drift. That is below reliable phase-identification support, so the final inventory remains three phases. I’m adopting the coordinate-refined project now, then checking every active/fixed profile term, contacts, fractions, and the exact observation grid before export.

The exact final replay remains converged at Rwp 3.887% (Rp 3.108%, GOF 1.734), and the observed/calculated profiles track peak positions, widths, and asymmetry across low, middle, and high angle without a new coherent residual family. The requested CIFs, profile, and JSON are exported from that exact project; I’m doing independent CIF/grid/schema validation and writing the short uncertainty report.

Completed and validated a three-phase model:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| CaWO₄ (scheelite) | 78.603 ± 0.078 wt% |
| Monoclinic WO₃ | 11.583 ± 0.049 wt% |
| Monoclinic Ca₃WO₆ prototype | 9.814 ± 0.057 wt% |

Final fit: Rwp 3.887%, Rp 3.108%, GOF 1.734. The Ca₃WO₆ structure is prototype-based and therefore less certain than its formal fraction uncertainty suggests.

